WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.870
(gentle music)

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:02.370
- [Narrator] Perhaps the
most resilient building

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:03.740
in the world can be found

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:05.540
in the Yangtze River in China,

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:08.467
where it has stood for the past 700 years.

00:00:08.467 --> 00:00:11.050
(gentle music)

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.340
Guanyin Pavilion, also known

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:18.190
as the Goddess of Mercy Pavilion,

00:00:18.190 --> 00:00:20.330
is a temple located in Ezhou City

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:22.013
in China's Hubei province.

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.960
Its origins date back to the Yuan Dynasty,

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:27.520
and is the only temple built

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:30.583
on the nearly 4,000-mile
long Yangtze River.

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:34.920
The temple sits on a large
reef rock in the river.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.200
During the rainy season,

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.580
the temple's foundation
is submerged in water,

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.730
and the building looks
as if it's floating.

00:00:42.490 --> 00:00:45.080
Today, the pavilion
isn't open to the public.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:47.350
However, that doesn't
stop adventure swimmers

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:48.800
from exploring the structure.

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:51.420
While floods and heavy rainstorms

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:54.440
have washed away trees and
more modern structures,

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:57.020
Guanyin Pavilion still stands strong.

00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:59.700
It's a testament to the
architectural engineering

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:00.999
of its ancestors.

00:01:00.999 --> 00:01:03.582
(gentle music)

00:01:04.971 --> 00:01:07.971
(soft mellow music)

